{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793345009,"nonce":"AJgFjzaVGCiq1JSpprDsIAv8oiE=","title":"Reuse optimized AOT bodies with context-aware cache keys","message":"Ash's recent preparation work caches lowered and optimized AIR separately and bounds live preparation state. Rayzor's BLADE already caches lowered MIR, but AotCompiler still repeats tree shaking, cross-module linking and MIR optimization after loading that MIR. The current audit removes an unconditional deep clone by taking owned MIR modules and adds separate MIR/LLVM/codegen effort controls; it does not establish a reusable post-link optimized-body cache.\n\nDesign an optimized-body artifact keyed by compiler build id, target/ABI and defines, optimization policy, source and transitive dependency fingerprints, type/layout and virtual-dispatch identities, and tree-shake reachability context. Do not write specialized or linked global ids into a source-only BLADE slot. Validate uncached/cold/warm parity and bounded memory independently of execution performance. Stage profiling via RAYZOR_AOT_PROFILE can identify whether this cache pays for its serialization and validation costs.\n\nRayzor's normal WebAssembly pipeline emits WASM directly and runs Binaryen; Ash's LLVM-WASM fixed per-function shard weight has no equivalent stage in that path. Revisit shard weighting only for an actual LLVM-WASM AOT path rather than applying it to native or direct-WASM compilation.","files":null}]}